#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129892} :activity/id #uuid "87e23de1-746c-5124-9751-bee5befcb28f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048554 :topic/id #uuid "a93bb5a8-5344-5f11-a0fd-45410668ecd6" :topic/math-academy-id 1984} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130327 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to compute the tangent plane to the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k,$ where $k$ is a constant, at some point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0).$ \n\nAs an example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2 +y^2+z.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that we wish to find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=  (1,1, 2).$ \n\nEvaluating $f$ at the point $(1,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = (1)^2 +(1)^2+2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of our level surface is\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at $(1,1,2),$ we can use the following result:\n\n> *Given a hypersurface $w = f(x,y,z)$ and a point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0,y_0,z_0)$ that lies in the domain of $f$, the gradient $\\nabla \\! f (\\mathbf x_0)$ is normal to the tangent plane of the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k$ at the point $\\mathbf x_0$*.\n\n![](images/11/1140e80fbb2efd684311cb7625e5b0be361d6fb951036253f57b9e51234de3ee.png)\n\nWe can now find the equation of the tangent plane to our level surface.\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf x_0 = (1,1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2) & =2(1)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+2\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,  we write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\nabla f(1,1,2) \\cdot \\langle x-1, \\: y-1, \\: z-2 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\langle 2,\\:2,\\:1\\rangle\\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1, z-2\\rangle &= 0\\[3pt]\n      2(x-1)+ 2(y-1) + (z-2) & =0\\\n      2x +2y +z -6&=0   \n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f7db883-9d6e-5a67-97d4-20274467a1e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "13f10d0e-ddd7-5833-b73f-b3f00ff7016a" :step/math-academy-id 11201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129893}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129079 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128893 :question/id #uuid "09499f38-a497-5f59-9cb2-0882a5853320" :question/math-academy-id "e-3368"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da47f01b-0db0-586f-9d55-5bd4c952afb6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394071}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent to Plane a Level Surface"} :step/id #uuid "6d9d3ca6-ae9b-5ac5-a35f-80fa6628ef2f" :step/math-academy-id 11204 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128998 :question/id #uuid "9091e2dc-5b05-5ae7-a498-9de0483ec375" :question/math-academy-id "e-6183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323850} 